@page
@model WoodruffDev.Pages.Services.ProjectBasedModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Project-Based Contracts";
}

<div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
        <span class="section-label">Service</span>
        <h1>Project-Based Contracts</h1>
        <p>Scoped, owned, and delivered&mdash;with written trade-offs and a clean handoff.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="service-detail fade-in">
            <h2>Service Overview</h2>
            <p>Some work doesn&rsquo;t fit a retainer and can&rsquo;t wait for a hire. You need a defined outcome, a firm timeline, and one person who owns it end-to-end. Project-Based Contracts are for the work your team shouldn&rsquo;t have to steal cycles from roadmap to get done&mdash;architecture assessments, modernization plans, migration strategies, or technical due diligence with a deadline attached.</p>
            <p>Every engagement starts with a written scope, a fixed fee or capped budget, and a named deliverable. Mid-project change orders are explicit, not implied.</p>

            <h2>Signals This Fits</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You need an outside voice to own a defined piece of work&mdash;not just advise on it</li>
                <li>A migration, rewrite, or modernization has stalled and needs a plan that survives contact with reality</li>
                <li>An investor, acquirer, or auditor is asking for technical due diligence your team can&rsquo;t produce quickly</li>
                <li>You have a decision to make but lack the architectural baseline to make it</li>
                <li>You want a deliverable you can hand to the next team or the next investor without edits</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Common Project Types</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Architecture assessments and modernization roadmaps</li>
                <li>Cloud migration strategy and execution playbooks</li>
                <li>API design, versioning, and platform-consolidation plans</li>
                <li>Legacy system decomposition and strangler-pattern sequencing</li>
                <li>Performance audits with ranked, actionable recommendations</li>
                <li>Technical due diligence for acquisitions, investments, or partnerships</li>
                <li>Post-incident architectural reviews that go beyond the blameless postmortem</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>How It Works</h2>
            <h3>Step 1 &ndash; Scope</h3>
            <p>We co-author a one-page scope: the question, the deliverable, the timeline, the access I need, and what&rsquo;s explicitly out of scope. You sign off before any clock starts.</p>
            <h3>Step 2 &ndash; Execute</h3>
            <p>I run the work with weekly status notes, an open Slack channel, and a mid-project checkpoint. No surprise invoices, no drifting deliverables.</p>
            <h3>Step 3 &ndash; Deliver &amp; Hand Off</h3>
            <p>Final deliverable, a walkthrough with your team (recorded), and a 30-day follow-up window for clarifying questions. Your team can run with the output on day one.</p>

            <h2>What You Get</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>A written scope with a fixed fee or a clearly capped budget</li>
                <li>Weekly status notes so leadership isn&rsquo;t chasing updates</li>
                <li>The agreed deliverable&mdash;assessment, plan, architecture spec, migration playbook, or due-diligence report</li>
                <li>Recorded walkthroughs your team can re-watch and onboard new engineers with</li>
                <li>A 30-day follow-up window for questions after final delivery</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Typical Timelines</h2>
            <p>Most projects run <strong>4&ndash;12 weeks</strong>. Due-diligence sprints can be as short as a week; platform-scale assessments can stretch to a quarter. I&rsquo;ll tell you up front if your timeline is unrealistic&mdash;not at the end of the engagement.</p>

            <div class="service-cta">
                <h3>Have a defined problem and a deadline?</h3>
                <p>Let&rsquo;s scope it properly and get you a deliverable you can act on.</p>
                <a href="/Contact" class="btn btn-primary">Scope a Project <span class="btn-arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
            </div>

            <div class="service-nav">
                <a href="/Services/MicroConsulting">&larr; Micro-Consulting</a>
                <a href="/Services/DevRel">Developer Relations &rarr;</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
